# What must never enter the build context.
#
# This file did not exist, and its absence broke production in three ways at
# once — none of which the build, the image, or the healthcheck complained
# about. `COPY . .` in the build stage runs AFTER `COPY --from=deps
# node_modules`, so everything here was copied over the top of it.
#
# 1. `node_modules` from the developer's macOS replaced the Linux one that
#    `npm ci` had just installed. The image shipped
#    @node-rs/argon2-darwin-arm64 and no Linux binary, so argon2 died with
#    "Failed to load native binding" — meaning nobody could sign in. The
#    healthcheck still answered {"ok":true}, because /api/health never hashes
#    a password.
# 2. `.env` — the local database password — was baked into the image. It also
#    won at runtime: the admin scripts pass `--env-file-if-exists=.env`, so
#    `docker compose exec app npm run db:migrate` read 127.0.0.1:3307 instead
#    of the compose variables.
# 3. `arquivos/` would have carried the client's uploaded files into every
#    image built from this directory.

# Installed inside the image by `npm ci`, for the right platform.
node_modules

# Built inside the image by `npm run build`.
.next
*.tsbuildinfo

# Secrets. `.env.exemplo` is the template and carries none, so it stays.
.env
.env.*
!.env.exemplo

# The client's uploaded files. They live on a bind mount, never in an image.
arquivos
files

# Not needed to build or run the server.
test
coverage
.git
.gitignore
README.md
AGENTS.md
CLAUDE.md
docker-compose.yml
compose.dev.yml
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
